# Learn more https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/getting-started-with-git/ignoring-files

# dependencies. No trailing slash, deliberately: `node_modules/` matches a
# directory and not a *symlink* named node_modules, so a link into another
# checkout's modules — once the way to run tests in a git worktree — was
# not ignored, was picked up by `git add -A`, and replaced the real directories
# with links to themselves on the next checkout. That link is no longer the way
# and is now refused: `bin/worktree-setup` installs into the worktree instead.
# The missing slash still matters, for any link that predates it.
node_modules

# Expo
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
expo-env.d.ts

# The two web app trains, built by bin/deploy-web from a tag and rsynced to the
# box. Ignored for the same reason server/web/dist is: a compiled artefact in
# the tree drifts from its source and nobody notices, because nobody reads
# minified output and its diffs are unreviewable. The mechanical half matters
# too — bin/deploy refuses a dirty tree, and a tracked bundle would dirty it on
# every build, so the guard would be passed over habitually and stop being
# worth having. See server/web/build.mjs and planning/WEB.md.
server/web/stable/
server/web/beta/

# Native
.kotlin/
*.orig.*
*.jks
# The Android upload key, which is a PKCS12 store and so named `.keystore`
# rather than `.jks`. It lives in ~/.config/thefloor and never in the tree —
# see planning/CREDENTIALS.md — and this pattern is the second line of defence
# rather than the first.
*.keystore
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision

# Metro
.metro-health-check*

# debug
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# macOS
.DS_Store
*.pem

# local env files
.env*.local

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# generated native folders — regenerated by `expo prebuild`, and the iOS one
# is ~1GB with Pods. Anchored patterns missed these once they moved into app/.
ios/
android/

# ...but a *local Expo module* keeps its hand-written native source in a
# directory called `ios`, and the unanchored rule above swallows it. That cost
# build 61 on 2026-08-20: `git add -A` committed the module's config and its
# TypeScript and silently dropped the Swift and the podspec, so the module was
# never autolinked, the build succeeded, and the diagnostic it existed for read
# "route unreadable" on the phone.
#
# The directory has to be un-ignored, not just its contents: git does not
# descend into an excluded directory, so `!app/modules/*/ios/**` alone would
# never be consulted.
!app/modules/*/ios/

# The same again for Android, added 2026-09-03 with `app/modules/call-service`
# — the foreground service that keeps a channel alive off screen, whose whole
# implementation is Kotlin and a manifest under a directory called `android`.
# Identical trap, identical cost, and it would have been quieter this time: a
# module with no native half links to nothing, so `startCallService` answers
# `false` exactly as it does on iOS, and the only symptom would be Android
# calls dying in the background again.
#
# Gradle's own output has to go back out, since the rule that was catching it
# is the one being negated.
!app/modules/*/android/
app/modules/*/android/build/

# editor backups
*~

# server data
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# local env — the wildcard matters: a .env.bak or .env.save is just as
# secret-bearing as .env itself, and the bare pattern would not have caught it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# scratch dev diagnostics (mint tokens, inspect live rooms)
dev-*.mjs

# what bin/deploy stamped on the way out — a fact about one box, written fresh
# every deploy. Tracking it would make every deploy dirty the tree it reports
# the cleanliness of.
server/deployed.json

# editor lock files
.#*
#*#

# git worktrees, which live inside the repo so they are easy to find. Each holds
# its own .git file, so `git add -A` in the main checkout does not merely add
# noise — it warns about an embedded repository and stages the worktree as a
# gitlink, which is never what was meant.
.claude/worktrees/
